(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-drill-classes"]={"title":"Drug Classes Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which class must be separated from antacids, iron and dairy because it chelates with cations?","choices":["Tetracyclines","Macrolides","Penicillins","Cephalosporins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They chelate with cations, so the dose has to be spaced away from antacids, iron and dairy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which class must be avoided in children under eight years old?","choices":["Macrolides","Tetracyclines","Cephalosporins","Penicillins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Under eight, because of tooth discolouration and depressed skeletal growth. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which class is used with caution in patients under eighteen?","choices":["Macrolides","Cephalosporins","Fluoroquinolones","Aminoglycosides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Caution under 18, because of the tendon and cartilage effects. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Dizziness, insomnia and somnolence — central nervous system toxicity — belong to which class?","choices":["Tetracyclines","Cephalosporins","Polymyxins","Fluoroquinolones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Central toxicity sits alongside the tendon rupture and peripheral neuropathy. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Gastrointestinal upset that is worst with erythromycin, especially in children and young adults, points to which class?","choices":["Macrolides","Penicillins","Tetracyclines","Aminoglycosides"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nausea, diarrhoea and abdominal pain, worse with erythromycin than with the newer members. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Interstitial nephritis with haematuria and crystalluria belongs to which class?","choices":["Macrolides","Aminopenicillins","Aminoglycosides","Fluoroquinolones"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alongside hepatic dysfunction, Clostridium difficile infection and the hypersensitivity spectrum. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which class is mostly renally eliminated, so its dosing changes with kidney function?","choices":["Macrolides","Allylamines","Cephalosporins","Topical corticosteroids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mostly renally eliminated — which is why the interval is stretched in renal impairment. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Tachycardia, headache, insomnia, low potassium and low magnesium describe which antifungal class?","choices":["Azoles","Polyenes","Allylamines","Echinocandins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Caspofungin, micafungin and anidulafungin, which also cause blood dyscrasias. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which antifungal class is used in the febrile neutropenic patient who is not responding to antibiotics?","choices":["Echinocandins","Allylamines","Topical azoles","Topical antifungals"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Given when antibiotics have not worked, and for aspergillosis that has failed itraconazole or amphotericin. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which class treats athlete's foot, ringworm, jock itch and onychomycosis as well as the systemic mycoses?","choices":["Echinocandins","Azoles","Polyenes","Topical corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The breadth is the point: superficial tinea through to Candida, Cryptococcus, Blastomyces, Histoplasma, Coccidioides and Aspergillus. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"Use of which class is expected to rise because of increasing multidrug resistance?","choices":["Penicillins","Macrolides","Polymyxins","Tetracyclines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Old, toxic, and coming back because gram-negative resistance is leaving fewer options. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which class covers gram-negative organisms only?","choices":["Macrolides","Cephalosporins","Allylamines","Polymyxins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gram-negative only, which is why they are reserved for resistant gram-negative infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Anxiety, tremor and headache, with poor penetration into the central nervous system, describe which class?","choices":["Catecholamines","Alpha-1 blockers","Beta-1 selective blockers","Antimuscarinics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, isoproterenol and dopamine — peripheral effects dominate because they do not cross well. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which class is used during orthopaedic surgery for fracture alignment and dislocation correction?","choices":["Cholinergic agonists","Nondepolarizing blockers","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Antimuscarinics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adjuvants in anaesthesia — intubation, and relaxing muscle enough to reduce a fracture or dislocation. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Poisoning by which group produces EITHER nicotinic OR muscarinic signs?","choices":["Alpha-1 blockers","Beta blockers","Organophosphates","Nondepolarizing blockers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Either picture can dominate, which is why the presentation varies so much. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which vehicle makes a topical corticosteroid MOST potent?","choices":["Cream","Lotion","Gel","Ointment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ointment beats cream, which beats lotion — the vehicle changes the potency, not just the feel. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which agent inactivates tretinoin if the two are applied together?","choices":["Benzoyl peroxide","Clindamycin","Azelaic acid","Tacrolimus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Benzoyl peroxide inactivates tretinoin, so they are applied at different times of day. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which class is losing efficacy over time because of Propionibacterium acnes resistance?","choices":["Topical retinoids","Topical antibiotics","Topical corticosteroids","Topical immunomodulators"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Which is why they are paired with benzoyl peroxide rather than used alone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which class produces salivation, flushing, falling blood pressure, abdominal pain, diarrhoea and bronchospasm?","choices":["Nondepolarizing blockers","Alpha-1 blockers","Cholinesterase inhibitors","Antimuscarinics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The muscarinic picture from raising acetylcholine at every synapse. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"“Dry as a bone, blind as a bat, red as a beet, mad as a hatter” describes toxicity from which class?","choices":["Cholinesterase inhibitors","Catecholamines","Nondepolarizing blockers","Antimuscarinics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The anticholinergic toxidrome, each phrase naming one blocked muscarinic effect. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which class requires less frequent dosing as renal function falls?","choices":["Penicillins","Macrolides","Allylamines","Topical retinoids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Renally cleared, so the interval stretches as clearance drops. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Hepatic dysfunction with jaundice and raised transaminases belongs to which penicillin group?","choices":["Penicillinase-resistant penicillins","Aminopenicillins","Cephalosporins","Carbapenems"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hepatic dysfunction, hepatitis and jaundice with raised AST, ALT, bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which class is second line for atopic dermatitis, after topical steroids?","choices":["Topical retinoids","Topical antibiotics","Topical immunomodulators","Topical antifungals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tacrolimus and pimecrolimus, used when steroids are not appropriate to continue. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which class must be applied with a warning about severe sunburn?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids","Topical immunomodulators","Topical antibiotics","Topical retinoids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Photosensitivity and severe sunburn, which is why they are applied at night and paired with sun protection. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which class causes bronchospasm and so must be used cautiously in reactive airway disease?","choices":["Cholinergic agonists","Antimuscarinics","Beta-2 agonists","Alpha-1 blockers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bronchospasm is the reason for caution — the airway constricts as secretions rise. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 31"}]};
